    "name": "Extracurricular Reading",
    "text": "3\nExtracurricular Reading\nInstructions\n\nEvery day, translate elevated reading material from outside the LSAT, using the following sources:\n\nScienceDaily.com\n\nIdeal daily reading for those who feel alienated by scientific language or fear science passages in RC\n\nBe sure to choose the articles that challenge you the most, not articles that look familiar or interesting\n\nAeon.co\n\nThe most similar articles to LSAT RC on the internet\n\nReading an Aeon.co article every day is a great habit if you have no specific topic-based weaknesses, but would like to make RC-style reading a habit.\n\nALDaily.com\n\nIdeal daily reading for those who feel alienated by abstract language, art, humanities, and pretension.\n\nBe sure to choose the articles that look the most unfamiliar, not articles that speak to your pre-existing interests.\n\nNYT Guide to Essential Knowledge\n\nThe single best way to fix gaps in common knowledge\n\nHere is a list of sections in the Guide that are relevant to LSAT. If any of these topics would trouble you as an RC passage, read over that section of the guide.\n\nLSAT Relevant Guide Sections: Architecture, Zoology, Botany, History, Art, Photography, Literature, Music (the serious parts about jazz and blues, especially), Film, Dance, Drama, Climate & Weather and Glossary of Weather Words (In Geography), Government, Law, Philosophy, Biology, Chemistry, Physics, Astronomy, Geology\n\nCrash Course\n\nNot a written source, but Crash Course is too useful for solving common knowledge gaps to not include\n\nWatch Crash Course while you're in line, waiting for someone, on hold, brushing your teeth, etc. Any time you could be scrolling on your phone, watch Crash Course instead.\n\nTop Five LSAT Relevant Crash Course Sections: Intellectual Property (Ellen's favorite for LSAT students), Economics, US Government & Politics, Art History, and Philosophy\n\nSEE FULL WORKOUT DETAILS",
